'''ECafè, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|ECafè}}) is a Balboan delivery services company providing food delivery, package delivery, grocery delivery, and last-mile courier services in the principal Balboan metropolitan areas. Headquartered in the [[Canal, Dàrsena|Canal]] neighborhood of [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company operates through a mobile application that connects customers with restaurants, grocery stores, and retail merchants, and dispatches a fleet of freelance couriers to fulfil orders. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker ECF.

ECafè was founded in 2015 and listed on the Bolsa in 2021. The company's listing was one of the most anticipated in the Bolsa's history, and its share price surged on the first day of trading, producing a market capitalisation that was widely regarded as disproportionate to its revenue and earnings. As of 2026, ECafè has never reported an annual profit, and its market capitalisation — approximately $5.0 billion — remains a subject of debate among Balboan investors and financial commentators, who disagree on whether the company's growth trajectory justifies its valuation or whether it represents a speculative bubble akin to the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 commodity trading bubble]].

== History ==

=== Founding (2015) ===

ECafè was founded in 2015 by [[Tân Hô-bûn]], a software engineer, and [[Jordi Estrada i Renom]], an entrepreneur, both graduates of the [[University of Sant Cristòfor]]. The two had met as students and developed the concept for a food delivery platform during their final year. The company was incubated by the [[Iniciativa del Fons Tecnològic de Balboa]], the government-backed technology investment fund that provided seed funding, office space, and mentorship to early-stage Balboan technology startups.

The company's concept was straightforward: a mobile application that allowed customers to order food from restaurants that did not offer their own delivery services, with a freelance courier dispatched to collect the food from the restaurant and deliver it to the customer's address. The model had been proven in Zong and Volisania, where food delivery platforms had grown rapidly in the 2010s, and the founders identified Balboa — where the restaurant sector was large and growing but delivery infrastructure was limited — as an underserved market.

The company's name, ECafè, was chosen to evoke the Balboan café culture while signalling the digital ("e-") character of the platform. The application launched in Sant Cristòfor in 2016, initially with a limited selection of restaurants and a fleet of approximately 200 couriers.

=== Growth (2016–2020) ===

ECafè grew rapidly in its first years, expanding from Sant Cristòfor to Portnou (2017), Sant Llàtzer (2018), and Sant Bart (2019). The company's growth was driven by aggressive marketing — including first-order discounts, referral bonuses, and restaurant subsidies that reduced the commission rate for the first year of partnership — and by the expansion of the courier fleet, which grew from 200 in 2016 to 5,000 by 2019.

The growth, however, was expensive. The company's marketing and subsidy programmes consumed more capital than its commission revenue generated, and the company was dependent on successive rounds of venture capital funding to finance its expansion. The [[Iniciativa del Fons Tecnològic de Balboa]] provided the initial seed funding, and subsequent rounds attracted Zong and Volisanian venture capital firms that were seeking exposure to the Balboan technology sector.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 accelerated ECafè's growth, as the lockdowns and restrictions on restaurant dining produced a surge in demand for food delivery. The company's order volume tripled between March and December 2020, and it expanded its courier fleet to 8,000 to meet the demand. The pandemic also prompted the company to expand into grocery delivery and package delivery, as the restrictions on movement created demand for home delivery of essential goods.

=== IPO and post-listing (2021–present) ===

ECafè was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in March 2021, at the height of the pandemic-driven demand for delivery services. The initial public offering was priced at $18 per share, valuing the company at approximately $2.5 billion. The share price surged 85 per cent on the first day of trading, closing at $33.30 and producing a market capitalisation of approximately $4.6 billion — a valuation that was more than 15 times the company's 2020 revenue of $290 million.

The post-listing period has been characterised by continuing revenue growth but persistent losses. The company's revenue grew from $290 million in 2020 to $680 million in 2026, but it has never reported an annual profit: the costs of courier recruitment, marketing, technology development, and customer acquisition have consistently exceeded the commission revenue. The company's net loss in 2026 was $95 million, an improvement from the $180 million loss in 2022 but still a significant deficit.

The company's defenders argue that the losses are a necessary investment in market share, and that the Balboan delivery market — which is still growing as internet penetration and digital payment adoption increase — will eventually produce sufficient revenue to cover the company's costs. The company's critics argue that the fundamental economics of food delivery — the high cost of last-mile logistics, the low commission rates that restaurants will accept, and the competition from the restaurants' own delivery operations — make profitability unlikely, and that the company's valuation is a speculative bubble that will eventually deflate.

The debate has produced significant volatility in the company's share price, which has ranged from a low of $12 in 2023 — when the company announced a larger-than-expected quarterly loss — to a high of $42 in 2025, when the company reported its first quarterly operating profit in the Sant Cristòfor market. As of 2026, the share price trades at approximately $36, implying a market capitalisation of $5.0 billion.

== Operations ==

ECafè operates across four principal service lines:

* '''Food delivery''': The company's original and largest service, connecting customers with approximately 2,800 restaurants across the four metropolitan areas it serves. The company charges restaurants a commission of 15 to 25 per cent per order and charges customers a delivery fee of $2.50 to $5.00.
* '''Grocery delivery''': The company partners with grocery stores and supermarkets to offer home delivery of groceries, a service that was launched during the pandemic and has continued to grow. The grocery delivery service competes with the supermarkets' own delivery operations.
* '''Package delivery''': The company offers same-day and next-day package delivery for retail merchants and e-commerce platforms, competing with [[Intercon Mercaderies]]' last-mile delivery division.
* '''Last-mile courier''': The company offers a general courier service for documents and small parcels, primarily serving the Sant Cristòfor business district.
* '''ECartera''': The company's peer-to-peer payment and digital banking platform, launched in 2019. ECartera allows users to send and receive money, pay for ECafè orders, store balances, and make purchases at participating merchants through a mobile wallet. The platform was developed to reduce the company's dependence on third-party payment processors and to capture the transaction data generated by the delivery business. ECartera has grown rapidly, reaching approximately 1.2 million registered users by 2026, and has become a significant source of revenue through transaction fees and merchant processing charges. The platform's expansion into digital banking — including savings accounts and micro-loans — has brought ECafè into regulatory territory traditionally occupied by the licensed banks, producing tensions with the [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]] over the scope of the company's financial activities.

The company's courier fleet consists of approximately 8,200 freelance couriers, who are classified as independent contractors rather than employees. The courier classification has been a source of legal and political controversy.

== Controversies ==

=== Courier classification ===

ECafè classifies its couriers as independent contractors, arguing that the flexibility of the platform — couriers can choose when and where to work, and are not assigned fixed shifts or routes — makes them independent businesses rather than employees. The classification allows the company to avoid paying the minimum wage, health insurance, and other employment benefits that would be required if the couriers were classified as employees.

The classification has been challenged by the [[Balboan Workers' Union]], which has filed complaints with the [[Balboa Labour Authority]] arguing that the couriers are de facto employees who are subject to the company's control through its algorithmic dispatch system, performance metrics, and disciplinary procedures. The Labour Authority issued a preliminary ruling in 2024 finding that the couriers were "economically dependent" on ECafè and that the contractor classification was inconsistent with the reality of the working relationship. The company has appealed the ruling, and the case is pending before the Labour Court.

The courier classification debate has become a political issue in Balboa, with the BPP's left wing calling for legislation that would presume platform workers to be employees unless the company could demonstrate genuine independence, and the Democratic Alliance arguing that such legislation would destroy the flexible work model that the platform economy provides.

=== Valuation debate ===

ECafè's market capitalisation — approximately $5.0 billion as of 2026, against a revenue of $680 million and a net loss of $95 million — has been a persistent subject of debate among Balboan financial commentators. The company's price-to-revenue ratio of approximately 7.4 is among the highest on the Bolsa, and its lack of profitability has drawn comparisons to the commodity trading bubble that preceded the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]].

The [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]] has not commented directly on ECafè's valuation, but the authority's 2024 annual report on market risks noted that "the valuation of certain technology-sector listings on the Bolsa appears to reflect expectations of future growth that may not be achievable, and investors should exercise caution." The report did not name ECafè specifically, but the reference was widely interpreted as being directed at the company.

